notes or  in IRAs under my plan  everyone presumptively would go into a  balanced account compose sixty percent  of a broad-based stock index fund and  forty percent of a broad-based  high-quality bond fund if you look at  that sort of account over thirty  thirty-five years it consistently  returns a little over eight percent  before inflation and if you take off  three percentage points for inflation  over five percent and the reason it does  it is because it's long-term consistent  investing it's dollar cost averaging yes  there are down years in the stock market  there were 13 down years between 1950  and 2004 but if you were investing over  a 35-year period in a broad-based index  and not in individual stocks that tends  to even out the set another thing is out  of those 13 years that the stock market  was down 12 of those years the bond  market was up so if you have a balanced  portfolio of stocks and bonds it tends  to be much less volatile so if we're  gonna do that sort of investing I  believe that we ought to do it in a  conservative manner as to whether rich  people should pay their fair share I  think you can argue about this left and  right what I'm trying to do is say let's  look at the total government support for  retirement income a lot of that  government tax subsidy in the IRAs and  401ks is now going to higher income  people and therefore their Social  Security benefits should slow have a  slower growth than lower wage workers  because the lower wage workers are not  getting most of the tax benefits from  IRAs and 401ks so I don't view this as  sucking the rich I view this as evening  out government support providing a  relatively neutral position of the  government relative to income groups  but to do that given the practical  context I think you have to treat  low-wage workers a little better in  terms of social security we have a  caller from Sterling Virginia for Robert  Posen hey there supposin hi how you done  to me it seems like most of the people  on the left their main focus is on  saving a government program and the  people on the right their main focus is  securing the financial foundation of our  seniors now to me I don't see why you  know there should be a fight that's  wasn't it too but whatever you hear the  Democrats talk on the floor of the  Senate or you know get in front of a  camera it's all about saving Social  Security whatever you hear the  Republicans talk it's all about making  sure the individual has enough money to  secure their financial freedom when  they're in their outer years and can no  longer be productive I don't see where  there's an argument about this  personally I think if you want to really  have ownership of Social Security you  need to do it this way have it to where  an individual can tap into their social  security fund way before the retirement  age say like when they get laid off  instead of you know destroying their  credit and you know all their bills  never being paid filing bankruptcy let  them tap into say $20,000 of their  social security fund every five years  with say a five percent penalty for  early withdrawal like if they get 20  grand actual and getting 19 and a  thousand is deducted from their fund and  then they can you know they can keep  making their payments they can secure  their credit score which is the true  you know catalyst for financial freedom  and then they can purchase like it's  maybe even first-time homebuyers that  are in lower income brackets can take  that 20,000 put it towards the purchase  of a home and that is their retirement  of owning your own home is the best  retirement fund you could ever get into  thanks mister posin well I tend to agree  that if you listen to Democrats and  Republicans they there there is  different language used than you would  think that they could come together but  I think what it really has to do with  is how each party views the way in which  you get to retirement income the  Democrats tend to like the traditional  Social Security program which is a  scheduled benefit like a defined benefit  plan in which there is no funding of an  individual account there is no  investment in any securities the  Republicans believe that you aren't  getting enough return out of your social  security investments because Congress as  I mentioned before hasn't really  invested the money and they think that  this sort of advanced funding is the  only way to go I think that those  aspects of the system and the  perspectives on that sort of are  maximizing the ideological differences  between the parties I think both parties  share the goal of trying to have a more  solvent system and when you get down to  the solvency of the system you have to  start talking about what's going to  happen to benefits what's going to  happen to retirement age what's going to  happen to revenues and if there were a  more pragmatic discussion on solvency I  believe that you'd have more coming  together of the parties as to your point  about having money taken out of Social  Security before retirement I'm afraid I  don't really agree with that we now  allow people to take money out of before  retirement in IRAs and 401ks but I think  we've found that some people take so  much money out then when it comes to the  time of retirement they don't have  enough now of course if they take their  money out and they invest it in a good  house and it appreciates well that's  great but we've seen many people take  money out before retirement and spend it  on various different things and then  when they get to retirement they don't  have enough money to live on and I think  that the ultimate goal the primary goal  of Social Security has to be to make  sure that everyone has a floor of  retirement income at that time and so I  believe that while it's okay to have  other programs to help the unemployed  and I would encourage them it's okay to  have and we do have programs to  encourage homebuyers you can't have  withdrawals from your IRAs and 401ks or  this purpose is  that's the social security I believe  that you really should leave it alone  until you retire so it's there so it's  make sure it's there  you know everyone it's hard to predict  for everyone where you're gonna be when  you're 65 so I think it's very important  that we keep that pot available just for  retirement Belpre Ohio you're on the air  with Robert Posen we're talking Social  Security good morning good morning mr.  Poe's anything thanks for taking my call  and I'm wondering when exactly was the  Social Security surplus tapped into and  why under which administration or was  there particularly administration and we  always hear the term locked box and I  thought this had applied to protecting  that Social Security surplus and I'm  also wondering you know I'm a single  mother of three making twenty seven  thousand a year I've never asked the  state or federal government for anything  in my whole life but I can't afford  health care for my children I can't even  afford catastrophic health care aren't  there more immediate issues right now to  be dealing with for the Bush  administration and for our Congress I  mean it just seems like a more immediate  crisis to be dealing with Thanks I  wanted to begin with the surplus  question yeah I would say this is  definitely a nonpartisan issue  Republican and Democratic  administrations in fact every president  we've had since 1983 has spent the  surplus and has given a special Treasury  I owe used to the trust fund the lockbox  was invoked for I think a total of about  six months beside having very good jokes  on Saturday Night Live it really never  came into existence unfortunately the  money never was locked up and the  lockbox I guess you would have to say is  empty as to the the relative merits of  addressing the social security versus  healthcare issues I I tend to agree that  health care is probably a more difficult  problem it's a it's a it's a it's a  major problem and it's surely one we  have to deal with but I think that if  you try to deal with health care which  is going to be a problem over the next  20 years  when you  you have not solved Social Security  you're going to be dealing with a very  large problem health care and a large  problem Social Security in Social  Security we actually have a chance to  fix this now  if we do some reasonable things now over  the next five to ten years we can start  moving in the right direction and so in  the out years we won't have this  albatross a lot on our back while we're  trying to deal with the very difficult  issues of health care so I don't view  them as mutually exclusive  I think it's an intelligent thing to try  to deal with the problem before it's a  crisis in a way that you can put it to  bed and then have the resources and the  time to tackle the healthcare problem  which probably as you say is going to be  something that may be more difficult  than social security I think they're  they're both problems that have to be  dealt with and I'd like to see us deal  with social security first get that off  the table and then concentrate on health  care and before we go mr. posed and back  to that subhead to the wall of the Wall  Street Journal piece today progressive  indexing your idea here may help Bush  broker a compromise what do you think do  you think this could be it  something that could propel this debate  forward well I think that progressive  indexing is an idea that appeals to  Democrats as well as Republicans because  its first and highest priority is to  protect low-wage workers like the woman  who was just on it was making twenty  seven thousand dollars a year but I  don't believe that progressive indexing  alone is going to be enough to get both  parties together I think it's critical  that we stop talking about the desert  personal accounts we stop talking about  this in a very ideological way and that  we start talking about solvency once we  start talking about solvency then I  think the parties will start to have  good discussions and let's leave as a  secondary question what will be the  sweeteners what will be the desserts  that will be packaged with any solvency  solution obviously President Bush wants  to have his form of personal accounts  there are a lot of other ideas that are  around we can have a reasonable  discussion about that sweetener about  personal accounts or add-on  carve-out accounts once we address the  solvency problem but if we aren't if we  don't put the solvency a question first  if we don't have that discussion which I  believe can be a bipartisan discussion  first then we're never going to get to  those other questions instead we're just  going to get embroiled as unfortunately  we have with a bit of ideological debate  a bit of stalemate and so I believe it's  not just progressive indexing that's the  key to getting this debate restarted  it's also getting to solvency as the  main focus if we can do solvency and  focus on that progressive indexing as a  reasonable approach I'm sure there are  other reasonable approaches that's the  way to get the debate going robert Posen  is chairman of MF s investment  management also a former member of the  president's Social Security Commission  back in 2001 and to joining us from  Boston thanks a lot for your time  thank you for having me
 

